  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-1846a502 Web logs overview`** (ID `cd3f8868-efff-4859-be92-4198e6787d11`)\n- Six panels, all ES|QL over `kibana_sample_data_logs`:\n  - Total requests (metric)\n  - 5xx responses (metric, `response.keyword LIKE \"5*\"`)\n  - Average response size in bytes (metric)\n  - Requests over time (line chart, bucketed on the dashboard time range)\n  - Requests by response code (pie)\n  - Top 10 URLs by requests (pie)\n- Default time range is last 7 days.\n\n**Rule: `EFF-1846a502 5xx spike`** (ID `42ee71a5-714f-4a12-84ce-6658d979bc92`)\n- Elasticsearch query rule (`.es-query`, DSL) on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`.\n- Query counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5??`, which covers 500\u2013599.\n- Runs every 1m, looks back 5m, fires when count > 10.\n- Enabled. The first scheduled run finished with `status: ok`.\n\n**Limits of what I verified**\n- Elasticsearch isn't reachable from here and Kibana's query proxies are disabled, so I couldn't confirm the response-code values or the 5xx count. The dashboard panels and rule query are unverified against real data.\n- The rule has no actions attached, so a firing rule records alerts in Kibana's Alerts view but sends no notification. Tell me if you want a connector added.\n\n**Choices I made**\n- Used `stackAlerts` as the rule consumer, following Kibana's ES query rule example.\n- Used a wildcard on `response.keyword` instead of a numeric range, because `response` is stored as a string.\n- Used pie charts for the breakdowns. The Dashboards API rejected ES|QL bar charts with a breakdown, so I used pies instead.\n\nThe payloads and API responses are in the scratchpad directory.",
